 turkey=[item('CA','Subway','6" Classics Subs — Turkey Breast'),item('CA','Subway','6-inch Fresh Fit — Turkey Breast'),item('MX','Subway','6-inch sub — Turkey Breast','MX|subway|1c5926505abe2837')]
 body='''<p>The listed Canadian Classics Turkey Breast is <strong>430 kcal and 20 g protein</strong>; the Canadian Fresh Fit Turkey Breast is <strong>420 kcal and 21 g protein</strong>; Mexico’s standard six-inch Turkey Breast is <strong>270 kcal and 15 g protein</strong>. The country and recipe family change the record even when the filling name sounds familiar.</p><h2>Three source-defined recipes</h2>'''+table(turkey,'Subway Canada June 2026 and Mexico November 2025: listed six-inch recipes')+'''<p>The Canadian Classics record is 233 g, Fresh Fit is 240 g and the Mexico sandwich is 205 g. Compared with Mexico’s standard sub, Canada’s Classics sub lists 160 kcal and 5 g protein more. This is an observed difference between documented recipes and portions, not a claim that one country uses an intrinsically better turkey.</p><h2>Why the filling name is not enough</h2><p>Subway Canada describes its Fresh Fit line as preset recipes on six-inch multigrain bread. Its Fresh Fit Turkey includes cheddar, vegetables and mayo. Those assumptions matter. A component labeled “Turkey Breast” contains only the named portion of turkey and cannot replace a complete sandwich in your diary.</p><h2>Use your ordering country first</h2><p>In Canada, select Classics or Fresh Fit according to what you ordered. In Mexico, use the Mexico source recipe. A country change does not translate one country’s ingredients into the other’s, and neither source verifies an unlisted customized sandwich.</p>'''+compare([turkey[0],turkey[2]],'Compare the Canadian and Mexican recipes')+'''<p>The edition dates differ. This table compares the retained source editions; it does not demonstrate a menu change over time or claim every newer recipe is already covered. Local availability and a current package or menu should be checked separately.</p>'''
